  cons "**Decoded containers, breaking computational hardness assumptions, and the sole unconditional floor.** Already-compressed containers (archives, media, serialized formats) appear high-entropy only to opaque byte scanners; because decoders are ordinary represented programs (Part I §§5, 8.6), lifting through a represented decoder recovers the underlying object for cross-object middle-out factoring. Likewise, cryptographic or pseudorandom outputs are not an unconditional mathematical boundary: a pseudorandom generator output G(s) of length 10⁹ from a 128-bit seed s has Kolmogorov complexity K(G(s)) ≤ 128 + O(1), and an encrypted structured message E_k(m) satisfies K(E_k(m)) ≤ K(m) + |k| + O(1). Treating such streams as incompressible rests solely on provisional, unproven computational hardness assumptions (one-way functions and average-case time-bounded Kolmogorov hardness K^t, kept explicitly as named premises in Part I §10.8). Hardness assumptions are meant to be broken, and only pure mathematical technology — discovering the hidden algebraic, geometric, or homotopy equivalence that collapses an apparently pseudorandom map into a short path — can break them; byte-heuristic and statistical compressors cannot touch that boundary. The sole unconditional mathematical floor is the counting (pigeonhole) theorem on genuinely structureless high-K bitstrings: among all 2^N strings of length N, fewer than 2^{N−k} admit an injective representation shorter than N − k bits. Real physical, biological, linguistic, and mathematical records occupy an infinitesimal, deeply relational corner of bitstring space far from that floor."
